# CODEOWNERS — owners of the repo's enforcement surfaces (#667)
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# The pin/version guards, the pipeline gate, and the CI workflows that run
# them are the surfaces that decide whether a PR is "compliant". Without an
# owner on those paths, a PR can author or weaken the guard that judges it in
# the same commit that needs it weakened, and the only trace is a diff a
# reviewer may or may not read (see #667; observed while landing #640).
#
# SCOPE: enforcement surfaces only. Documentation, content, and general
# source paths are deliberately NOT owned here (see the non-goal in #667 and
# the "Not guarded" section of docs/ops/guarded-paths.md).
#
# GitHub evaluates these gitignore-style patterns last-match-wins; any line
# with invalid syntax is silently skipped, so keep every line a valid pattern
# followed by at least one owner. The guarded path list is documented in one
# place — docs/ops/guarded-paths.md — and must match this file.
#
# NOTE (bootstrap ordering): GitHub reads CODEOWNERS from the PR's BASE branch,
# so this file has no effect on the PR that adds it — ownership starts with the
# next PR that touches a guarded path after this merges.
#
# NOTE (single-writer reality, #669): this file has NO OBSERVABLE EFFECT today.
# GitHub's only two CODEOWNERS behaviours are (1) auto-requesting a review and
# (2) blocking a merge when code-owner review is required — there is no third
# "annotation" behaviour; nothing is attributed, displayed or enforced. Here:
# GitHub never requests a review from the PR author and @daniel-ospina is the
# repo's only collaborator, so every PR is authored by the sole code owner
# (voids 1); and `required_pull_request_reviews` is null (voids 2). This file is
# a hook for the future, not a control today.
#
# Four settings would be needed to make it bind — all four, not three: raise
# `required_pull_request_reviews`, enable `require_code_owner_reviews` within it,
# add a SECOND approving identity (#669), and set `enforce_admins: true` — which
# is false today, so even with a second identity the sole admin could still merge
# their own PR via "Merge without waiting for requirements". That is an
# every-merge admin override, not a hard deadlock. #646 is unrelated throughout:
# it makes a code status check required, not a review.

# The ownership file itself — changing who owns the guards needs owner review.
/.github/CODEOWNERS                       @daniel-ospina

# CI workflows. pipeline-compliance.yml implements the repo's ONLY required
# status check (context: `pipeline-compliance`). Renaming its job is FAIL-CLOSED,
# not a silent disable: the required context is then never reported, so GitHub
# parks the PR at "Expected — Waiting for status to be reported" and the merge is
# blocked. The quiet bypass is editing the job body — or the script it runs — so
# it always exits 0. That is why workflow and gate edits need owner review.
# templates/ holds the materialization source for the reusable workflows —
# edit the template, not the copy, so it needs the same owner.
/.github/workflows/                       @daniel-ospina
/templates/.github/workflows/             @daniel-ospina

# The pipeline gate itself — this script decides every merge.
/scripts/check-pipeline-compliance.sh     @daniel-ospina

